CAT = "txadjContract"; SEG = "CON"
Q = [
("Before investigating a questionable claim, an adjuster has the insured sign a document stating that the investigation does not give up any of the insurer's policy rights. This is a:",
 "Non-waiver agreement", ["Reservation of rights", "Release of all claims", "Proof of loss"],
 "A non-waiver agreement is a two-party document signed by the insured. A reservation of rights is a one-sided letter from the insurer."),
("A reservation of rights letter differs from a non-waiver agreement because it:",
 "Is a one-way notice, unsigned", ["Admits the claim is covered", "Ends any duty to defend", "Must be filed with TDI"],
 "The insurer sends a reservation of rights letter on its own to keep its coverage defenses while it investigates or defends. The insured does not have to sign it."),
("The voluntary giving up of a known right is:",
 "Waiver", ["Estoppel", "Subrogation", "Indemnity"],
 "Waiver is the intentional relinquishment of a known right. Estoppel bars a party from asserting a right because of its conduct and the other party's reliance."),
("An adjuster tells an insured not to bother filing a proof of loss, and the insurer later denies the claim for lack of one. The insured's best argument is:",
 "Estoppel", ["Subrogation", "Liberalization", "Indemnity"],
 "The insured relied on the insurer's representative to their detriment, so the insurer may be estopped from enforcing the proof of loss condition."),
("A statement in a policy that the insured guarantees to be true, such as a promise to keep a sprinkler system in service, is a:",
 "Warranty", ["Representation", "Binder", "Declaration"],
 "A warranty is part of the contract and must be strictly true. A representation is a statement on the application believed true, and voids coverage only if material."),
("A lender holding a mortgage on a house has an insurable interest in it:",
 "Up to the unpaid loan balance", ["For the full value of the house", "Only after a foreclosure", "Only if it occupies the house"],
 "A mortgagee's financial stake, and so its insurable interest, is the unpaid debt secured by the property."),
("Who lacks an insurable interest in a rental house?",
 "A guest who stayed there once", ["The landlord who owns it", "The bank holding the mortgage", "A contractor with a lien"],
 "Insurable interest requires a financial loss if the property is damaged. Owners, mortgagees and lienholders have one; a former guest does not."),
("Under the standard mortgage clause, if the owner fails to file a proof of loss, the mortgagee must file one within how many days after the insurer's notice?",
 "60 days", ["10 days", "30 days", "90 days"],
 "The mortgage clause requires the mortgagee to render proof of loss within 60 days after notice of the owner's failure to do so."),
("Under the standard mortgage clause, before canceling the policy the insurer must notify the mortgagee at least:",
 "10 days before", ["Five days before", "30 days before", "On the cancellation date"],
 "The standard mortgage clause requires at least 10 days' written notice of cancellation to the mortgagee."),
("An insurer pays the mortgagee after denying the owner's claim because the owner set the fire. The insurer then:",
 "Steps into the lender's rights", ["Becomes the owner of the house", "Must also pay the owner in full", "Forgives the remaining loan"],
 "When the insurer pays the mortgagee but denies the owner, it is subrogated to the mortgagee's rights and can collect the debt from the owner."),
("A house with furniture still inside but no one living there is:",
 "Unoccupied", ["Vacant", "Abandoned", "Uninhabitable"],
 "Unoccupied means no one is living there though contents remain. Vacant means the building has neither occupants nor the contents needed to live or operate there."),
("Ending a policy at the end of its term, rather than during the term, is:",
 "Nonrenewal", ["Cancellation", "Rescission", "Suspension"],
 "Nonrenewal ends coverage at expiration. Cancellation ends it midterm, and rescission treats the policy as if it never existed."),
("When a material misrepresentation on the application is discovered, an insurer that treats the policy as never having existed has:",
 "Rescinded it", ["Canceled it", "Nonrenewed it", "Endorsed it"],
 "Rescission voids the contract from inception and usually returns the premium. Cancellation ends coverage only from a future date."),
("A binder that an agent gives over the phone is:",
 "Binding though oral", ["Void until written", "Good for one day only", "Binding on the agent only"],
 "A binder may be oral or written and binds the insurer to temporary coverage until the policy is issued or the binder is canceled."),
("In a commercial policy with several named insureds, which one is responsible for paying premiums and receives any return premium?",
 "The first named insured", ["Each named insured equally", "The producing agent", "The loss payee"],
 "The Common Policy Conditions make the first named insured responsible for premium payments and the one who receives return premiums."),
("In a liability claim, the insured's duty to cooperate means the insured must:",
 "Assist the defense as asked", ["Settle with the claimant", "Pay the defense lawyer", "Admit fault right away"],
 "The insured must cooperate in the investigation, settlement and defense, such as attending hearings and helping secure evidence. The insurer pays the defense."),
("A liability policy condition says that, except at its own cost, the insured must not voluntarily:",
 "Pay or promise to pay a claimant", ["Give first aid at the scene", "Report the claim to its agent", "Keep records of the accident"],
 "The voluntary payments condition protects the insurer's control of settlement. First aid given at the time of an accident is a stated exception."),
("Under most liability policies, the insurer's duty to defend:",
 "Is broader than its duty to pay", ["Ends once fault is admitted", "Applies only to proven claims", "Covers only suits in Texas"],
 "The duty to defend applies to any suit alleging facts that could be covered, even if groundless, so it is broader than the duty to indemnify."),
("The policy section that gives the meaning of words shown in quotation marks is the:",
 "Definitions", ["Declarations", "Conditions", "Insuring agreement"],
 "Words in quotation marks are defined terms, and their meanings are in the definitions section."),
("An insurer holding a Texas certificate of authority to write insurance in the state is:",
 "Admitted", ["Nonadmitted", "A surplus lines carrier", "Alien"],
 "An admitted (authorized) insurer holds a certificate of authority in the state. A nonadmitted insurer does not and writes there only through surplus lines."),
("Policies written by a nonadmitted insurer through a surplus lines agent are generally:",
 "Not guaranty fund protected", ["Rated by TDI before sale", "Limited to personal autos", "Free of all state tax"],
 "State guaranty associations protect policyholders of insolvent admitted insurers. Surplus lines policies generally fall outside that protection."),
("The principle that both parties to an insurance contract must be honest and disclose material facts is:",
 "Utmost good faith", ["Indemnity", "Insurable interest", "Proximate cause"],
 "Utmost good faith requires a higher standard of honesty than ordinary contracts, which is why concealment and misrepresentation can void coverage."),
("Because the insurer pays only if the insured meets duties such as giving notice and filing proof of loss, an insurance policy is a:",
 "Conditional contract", ["Unilateral contract", "Aleatory contract", "Executed contract"],
 "A conditional contract obliges the insurer to pay only if the insured performs certain acts, such as prompt notice, cooperation and proof of loss."),
("The rule that the written policy cannot be changed by oral statements made before it was signed is the:",
 "Parol evidence rule", ["Doctrine of estoppel", "Reasonable expectations rule", "Pro rata liability rule"],
 "The parol evidence rule treats the written contract as the full agreement, so earlier oral statements cannot change its terms."),
("Some courts read coverage as an ordinary policyholder would expect it, even when the fine print says otherwise. This is the:",
 "Reasonable expectations doctrine", ["Parol evidence rule", "Doctrine of waiver", "Proximate cause rule"],
 "The reasonable expectations doctrine, adopted in some states, honors the objectively reasonable expectations of the insured about coverage."),
("Under the ISO homeowners forms, the policy period begins and ends at:",
 "12:01 a.m. standard time", ["Noon, standard time", "Midnight, daylight time", "When premium is paid"],
 "ISO homeowners policies run from 12:01 a.m. standard time at the residence premises. The older Standard Fire Policy used noon."),
("A liability policy condition that applies coverage to each insured as if each had a separate policy, apart from the limits, is:",
 "Separation of insureds", ["Other insurance", "Liberalization", "Concealment or fraud"],
 "Separation of insureds (severability of interests) applies the policy separately to each insured against whom a claim is made, though the limits are shared."),
("Adding a newly bought vehicle or raising a limit in the middle of the policy term is done with a(n):",
 "Endorsement", ["Waiver", "Rescission", "Appraisal"],
 "An endorsement is a written change to the policy that adds, removes or alters coverage during the term."),
]
